<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Kamakau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Samuel Mānaiakalani Kamakau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Mānaiakalani Kamakau was a Hawaiian historian and scholar whose writings, published in Hawaiian-language newspapers and later compiled into books, became an invaluable resource on the Hawaiian people, culture and language. Born at Mokulēia on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>, he studied at Lahainaluna Seminary under Sheldon Dibble from 1833 and in 1841 helped form the first Hawaiian historical society, with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> as its president. From 1866 to 1871 he wrote serial histories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>, the House of Kamehameha and Hawaiʻi, while serving as a judge and legislator. They appeared in English as Ruling Chiefs of Hawaiʻi (1961) and Ka Poʻe Kahiko (1964).</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To invade Kauaʻi he built a fleet of peleleu war canoes, some 800 according to the historian <b>Samuel Kamakau</b>, only for an epidemic to break the expedition in 1804.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of what is known comes from the Hawaiian scholars David Malo, <b>Samuel Kamakau</b> and John Papa ʻĪʻī, writing after the kapu ended in 1819, and from European visitors’ journals.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li></ul></section>
